In Java, you can use a try-catch-finally block to handle exceptions and ensure resource cleanup. The finally block executes regardless of whether an exception was thrown.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class FileHandler {
    public void readFile(String path) {
        BufferedReader reader = null;
        try {
            reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path));
            System.out.println(reader.readLine());
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("An error occurred while reading the file: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            try {
                if (reader != null) {
                    reader.close();
                }
            } catch (IOException ex) {
                System.err.println("Failed to close the reader: " + ex.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

